  "image_name": "bea82d826f94fbdbef63394043d92cda.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two routes for making mixed-cation perovskite quantum dots by exchanging cations between FA-based and Cs-based particles. In the standard synthesis, separate FAPbI3 and CsPbI3 quantum dots, both capped with oleic acid ligands and containing some vacancies, are mixed and allowed to react for about 24 hours. The result is Cs1−xFAxPbI3 quantum dots in which the two cations become distributed throughout each nanocrystal, but the drawing suggests a relatively defect-rich structure with remaining vacancies.\n\nThe second route shows the same exchange carried out with excess oleic acid. Here the process is much faster, reaching mixed-cation dots in about 30 minutes. The particles appear more uniformly passivated by ligands, and the figure explicitly links this condition to lower defect density, longer carrier lifetime, and higher photovoltaic efficiency. Overall, the message is that adding extra oleic acid improves surface chemistry during cation exchange, speeds up the formation of mixed perovskite quantum dots, and produces higher-quality materials for solar-cell use.",
